"""s22-calld: voice calls without a GUI (D028).

Watches the modem's QMI Voice call status. An incoming call rings (the
vibrator, repeating) and posts a "call" notification with the caller's
number. It is answered by opening the lid or the Call key, and ended or
rejected by closing the lid or the End key (the power key, short press).
While a call is up, the call audio (the modem's voice session on the ADSP,
VoiceMMode1) is routed to the earpiece and the keypad microphone, and the
voice PCM is held open; it is undone when the last call ends.

Outgoing calls and control: `s22-call` and the Phone app talk to this
daemon over /run/s22-call.sock (root and group wheel): dial NUMBER,
answer, hangup, reject, dtmf DIGIT, mute on|off, status. Nothing is ever
dialled by the daemon itself.

For the Phone app: STATE has a "call ID DIRECTION STATE SINCE NUMBER" line
a call (SINCE: when it was answered, or first seen; epoch seconds) and
mute=0|1; each ended call is a line of LOG, "EPOCH<TAB>DIRECTION<TAB>
NUMBER<TAB>RESULT<TAB>SECONDS" (RESULT: answered, missed, rejected,
outgoing, unanswered). A ringing call posts a "call" notification whose
link (call:incoming) s22-shell opens at once; a missed one leaves a
"missed-call" notification. Names come from the contacts (CONTACTS).

Volume: the earpiece follows the call_volume setting (1-7, s22-wm changes
it with the volume keys); the microphone gets the call_mic_adc and
call_mic_digital settings' gain during a call (Settings > Developer
options, tried with s22-mictest; else CALL_MIC_ADC/CALL_MIC_DIGITAL from
/etc/conf.d/s22-call), back to 0 dB after.

Speakerphone: the speaker key (SPEAKER_KEY) or "speaker on|off|toggle"
moves the call's voice between the earpiece (Primary MI2S, the codec's
RX1) and the loudspeaker (Quinary MI2S, the AW88194), briefly reopening
the voice session, which takes its ports when it starts. On the speaker
the call volume drives the amp's "Speaker Volume"; the system volume
(the volume setting) is put back when the call ends. STATE has speaker=.

Ringtone: a ringing call plays the caller's ringtone (X-S22-RINGTONE in
the contacts), else the ringtone setting, else DEFAULT_RINGTONE, looping
through s22-play at the ring volume, unless sounds=off; "silence" (s22-wm
sends it on a volume key) stops the ringtone and the vibration. With
tts_calls=on (Settings, Accessibility, D065) s22-say says "Call from NAME"
(the contact, else the number digit by digit), the ringtone plays for
RING_SPELL seconds, and so on while the call rings.

Ring vibration (D042): the caller's call vibration (X-S22-CALLVIB), else
vib_ring, else 900 on, 600 off, repeated while the call rings; none with
vibrate=off; at vib_strength (1-15).

Ringback: while an outgoing call is alerting (the other phone rings) the
network sends no tone, so s22-ringback plays one on the earpiece beside
the voice path (MultiMedia1 on PRI_MI2S_RX).

Other sound (D085): while a call rings, every other stream in the GUI's
PipeWire is turned down by DUCK_DB from where it was; once a call has audio (answered, or
dialled), they are muted at once, the media players (MPRIS) playing are
paused, and streams that start meanwhile are muted as they come. When the
calls end, all is put back and the players paused here play again. Ours
(the ringtone, speech, these tones: s22-play, s22-calld) are left alone.
While a call has audio the card's media playback (MultiMedia1, under the
sound server's s22.speaker) goes where the voice goes, so the ringback and
the keypad's tones are heard at the ear; a digit sent ("dtmf") sounds its
DTMF tone there for DTMF_MS.

At the ear: while a call has audio, the proximity sensor is held from
s22-sensord and STATE says near=0|1; s22-wm turns the display off and
ignores touches while it is near.

Voicemail: the SIM's mailbox number (EF_MBI says which EF_MBDN record is
voicemail; read through qmicli at start) goes to VOICEMAIL for the Phone
app, and "voicemail" dials it.

Settings in /etc/conf.d/s22-call: CALL_ARGS, -v verbose, -L to disable
lid answer/hang-up.

  s22-calld [-v] [-L]
"""
